nical competence, calmness under pressure, decision-making, teamwork/communication, professional development.\n\nLet's assess each candidate:\n\nA: strengths: anticipates dangerous situations (reliability, safety), sees complex connections (cognitive), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (teamwork). Weaknesses: show-off (could affect crew morale), not open to new ideas (resistance to learning, professional development). No explicit calmness under pressure info, but anticipating dangerous situations suggests proactive, but not necessarily calm.\n\nB: strengths: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (calmness), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical). Weaknesses: weak memory for numbers (could affect navigation), makes nasty remarks (teamwork/crew cooperation), no mention of decision speed.\n\nC: strengths: makes correct decisions quickly (decision-making), keeps calm in crisis (calmness), understands complicated technology (technical). Weaknesses: difficulty communicating ideas (teamwork), egocentric (crew), not willing to further education (professional development). No mention of reliability.\n\nD: strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately (calmness), concentrates well, solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (reliability). Weaknesses: hot temper, arrogant, weak leadership, know-it-all (teamwork issues). No mention of weather assessment.\n\nWe need to weigh seriousness: For long-distance flights, calmness under pressure, decision-making, reliability, teamwork are critical. Technical competence also important.\n\nCandidate B seems strong on reliability and stress handling, but has interpersonal issue (nasty remarks) which could be severe for crew. Also weak memory for numbers could be safety risk.\n\nCandidate C has quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, technical understanding, but communication difficulty and egocentric attitude are big negatives for crew. Also not willing to further education.\n\nCandidate D has good problem solving, responsibility, but hot temper and arrogance could be dangerous under stress; also weak leadership.\n\nCandidate A has strong anticipation and leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas could affect crew and learning.\n\nWhich candidate balances? Possibly Candidate A: leadership, anticipation, spatial vision, but need calmness evidence. Not given, but anticipation may reduce need for calmness? Could be a gap.\n\nCandidate B: handles stress well, conscientious, but interpersonal negativity is serious.\n\nCandidate C: calm, quick decisions, but communication issues.\n\nCandidate D: problem solving, but temperament issues.\n\nWe might need more info: e.g., does Candidate A handle stress? Not known. Does Candidate B have good decision speed? Not known. Does Candidate C have reliability? Not known.\n\nWe could ask other agents for missing strengths: e.g., ask Agent 3 about Candidate A's calmness under pressure, or Candidate B's decision-making speed, etc.\n\nBut we need to produce a public message now. We can give provisional preference, maybe Candidate A seems balanced but need more info on calmness. Or Candidate B's interpersonal issue may be disqualifying.\n\nGiven the seriousness of teamwork, I think Candidate A's leadership and openness issues may be less severe than overt nasty remarks (B) or hot temper (D) or egocentric (C). Show-off may be less critical. So provisional preference: Candidate A.\n\nWe should state confidence moderate, and note uncertainties: lack of explicit calmness evidence for A, and unknown about B's decision speed, C's reliability, D's technical depth.\n\nWe can ask others for missing info in next round, but for now we give provisional.\n\nLet's craft public message."}
